meta {
  name: Create curriculum
  type: http
  seq: 18
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/curricula
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  X-School-Id: {{schoolCode}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "name": "Mathematics, Class 6",
    "code": "MATH-6-2025",
    "academic_session_id": "{{sessionId}}",
    "subject_id": "{{subjectId}}",
    "grade_level_id": "{{gradeLevelId}}",
    "board": "CBSE",
    "description": "Syllabus as taught this session.",
    "origin": "school"
  }
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 201
  res.body.status: eq draft
  res.body.origin: eq school
  res.body.code: eq MATH-6-2025
}

script:post-response {
  bru.setEnvVar("curriculumId", res.body.id);
}

docs {
  A curriculum is one subject, one grade, one session. All three are required
  and none of them can be changed afterwards: a syllabus that could be moved to
  another grade would take its nodes, its placements and every mark recorded
  against them with it.

  origin says where it came from. school means somebody here wrote it;
  imported means it came from a board's published syllabus. The distinction
  matters when the board revises: an imported curriculum can be replaced
  wholesale, a school's own cannot.

  Created draft on purpose. Request 21 activates it, and that is where the
  rule about one active syllabus per subject, grade and session bites.
}
